/* ============================================================================
   inflation-adjusted-value.epher -- translating sums of money across years
   at a steady inflation rate

   At yearly inflation i, prices multiply by (1+i)^n over n years. So a sum
   of money today buys, n years from now, what (1+i)^n times as many dollars
   buy today; and a sum quoted in n-year dollars is worth only 1/(1+i)^n as
   much in today's dollars. This script prints both directions for one sum.
   Source: standard finance formulas; the same deflation appears in
   Kellison, The Theory of Interest, chapter 1.

   Accuracy: the values print to 12 digits and are exact rationals of the
   knob values (the division 1000/1.03^10 does not repeat in a way the
   display keeps as a fraction, so it shows as a decimal).

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/finance/interest/inflation-adjusted-value.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\finance\interest\inflation-adjusted-value.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/finance/interest/inflation-adjusted-value.epher

   The knobs to edit are in the knob section; everything after it is
   machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- knobs ------------------------------------------------------------------
const amount = 1000         // the sum of money, USD
const inflation = 3%        // yearly inflation rate
const years = 10            // how many years the money moves

// ---- helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------
def inflate(a, i, n) = a * (1 + i) ^ n      // a today, in n-year dollars
def deflate(a, i, n) = a / (1 + i) ^ n      // a in n years, in today's dollars

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 1000 today keeps its buying power as 1343.92 of ten-years-from-now money:
// the price level rises 34.4% over the decade.
print("1000 today, in 10-year dollars:", inflate(amount, inflation, years))
// 1000 promised in 10 years is only 744.09 in today's money: divide by
// 1.03^10 (equivalently, multiply by 1.03^-10).
print("1000 in 10 years, in today's dollars:", deflate(amount, inflation, years))
print("price level multiplier:", (1 + inflation) ^ years)
/* ---- expected output ----
= 1000
= 0.03
= 10
= 1000 today, in 10-year dollars: 1343.91637934
= 1000 in 10 years, in today's dollars: 744.093914897
= price level multiplier: 1.34391637934
*/
